# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/005_straddling_and_zero_width.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_straddling_and_zero_width
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Bitfields straddling a byte boundary inside a storage unit, fields pushed to a fresh unit, and zero-width and unnamed separators
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The default undefined-behaviour warning gate applies unmodified, with no deviation:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror.  The program
was verified to pass that gate and to run cleanly under
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all.

No unspecified padding bit is ever observed, and this is the program's central safety property.
A bitfield struct's unnamed padding bits hold unspecified values even after every named field
has been assigned, because padding bits are not members and a member write is not required to
preserve them.  The program therefore prints a byte image only for the two structs whose named
field widths sum to a whole number of four-byte storage units: cover32 sums to exactly
thirty-two bits and cover64 to exactly sixty-four.  On every toolchain in the measured target set
that leaves no padding bit in either struct, which was confirmed directly from the printed sizes
and byte images rather than assumed; the C standard does not itself guarantee padding-free
adjacent allocation, so this is a measured property of the four target ABIs and not a derivation
from the standard.  Should a toolchain ever pack these structs differently, the byte-image lines
below would change and this record's expectations would have to be re-derived for that target
rather than read as a compiler defect.  Every struct that necessarily contains padding, which is
every struct demonstrating a zero-width or unnamed separator and the struct whose fields are each
pushed to a fresh unit, is observed only through sizeof, _Alignof and read-back of its named
members.  Both byte-image unions are additionally zeroed byte by byte through an explicit loop
before any bitfield is written, so the initial state is defined even before any field is stored.

No aliasing violation: the byte image is read exclusively through an unsigned char array member
of a union that also contains the bitfield struct, which is the well-defined aliasing route.  No
incompatible pointer type is ever formed or dereferenced, and no struct pointer is cast to any
other pointer type.

Nothing depends on unspecified evaluation order, and the volatile runtime variant is where that
property has to be established rather than assumed.  An access to a volatile object is an
observable side effect, and the relative order of side effects within one argument list is
unspecified, so a printf reading four volatile bit-fields in one call would have made the order
of four side effects depend on the unspecified order of argument evaluation.  Every volatile
field is therefore read into a plain local in a full expression of its own, each separated from
the next by a sequence point, and only those plain locals are passed: NO ARGUMENT TO ANY CALL IN
THIS PROGRAM HAS A SIDE EFFECT.  The staging changes nothing the program observes -- each value
is read exactly once, no read modifies the object another read names, and the printed bytes are
byte-identical to the unstaged form on all four targets at all three optimization levels -- and
it costs the test nothing, because a load from volatile storage is still unavailable for
compile-time substitution and every bit-field extraction is still emitted by the backend.

Every store is a constant inside the destination field's representable range, so no conversion
can change a value.  No arithmetic is performed on any bitfield, so no signed overflow can
occur.  No shift operator appears anywhere in the program, so no question of shift range,
negative left shift or implementation-defined negative right shift arises.  No object is
modified twice between sequence points.  No uninitialized read: the two automatic unions are
fully zeroed before use, and every file-scope object either has an explicit constant initializer
or has static storage duration with every field written before it is read.  No pointer value,
address, timestamp, random value or locale-dependent format is printed, and every loop bound is
a sizeof of a fixed-size array.

No call receives a side-effecting argument.  The runtime variant reads volatile bitfields, and
a volatile access is an observable side effect whose order relative to the other arguments of
the same call is unspecified, because C11 6.5.2.2p10 fixes no evaluation order among them.
Every such read is therefore staged into a plain local in a full expression of its own before
the printing call that reports it, and every volatile write likewise stands alone, so the order
of the accesses is a property of this program rather than of whichever compiler translated it.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every bitfield is declared with an explicit signed int or unsigned int base type, including the
unnamed separators, which are spelled as unsigned int with no name.  A plain int bitfield's
signedness is implementation-defined, so using it would manufacture a spurious divergence;
stating the signedness in the declaration removes that dependency entirely.

Plain char is not used as a value type.  The byte-image arrays are unsigned char, whose
signedness is fixed by the standard, which matters because plain char signedness was measured
to differ across the four targets (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64).

No value whose width varies by target is printed.  sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) differ on
i686 (4) from the other three targets (8), so neither is printed, and no pointer value or
address appears in the output.

THE GOLDEN SIZES, ALIGNMENTS AND BYTE IMAGES REST ON A MEASURED ABI PREMISE, NOT ON A
LANGUAGE GUARANTEE, and the distinction matters enough to state precisely.  C leaves almost
everything about bitfield storage implementation-defined: the size of the addressable unit
into which a bitfield is allocated, whether a field that does not fit may straddle two such
units, the order in which fields are allocated within a unit, and how much padding the
containing structure carries (C11 6.7.2.1p11, and 6.7.2.1p15 for the padding).  Nothing in
the standard derives a four-byte allocation unit from a four-byte int, so the values below
CANNOT be deduced from int's width, and this record does not claim they can.

What supports them is measurement.  On all four supported targets, with gcc 13.3.0 and
re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0, the following were observed to agree exactly:

  - the allocation unit for a bitfield declared on an int base type is FOUR BYTES;
  - allocation within a unit proceeds from the LEAST SIGNIFICANT BIT UPWARD, and all four
    targets are little-endian, so a field's bit position within a unit fixes which bytes of
    the structure image it occupies;
  - a field DOES straddle a BYTE boundary inside its allocation unit -- struct cover32's
    9-bit c occupies bits 8 through 16 and its 15-bit d occupies bits 17 through 31, which
    is what makes its printed byte image a non-trivial fact;
  - a field that does NOT fit in the remainder of the current unit STARTS A FRESH UNIT
    rather than straddling the unit boundary.  This is the opposite of what an earlier form
    of this record asserted, and the corpus already depends on the correct reading: struct
    nostraddle declares 20 + 20 + 24 bits, which would occupy 8 bytes if the unit boundary
    could be straddled and occupies 12 because it cannot -- and 12 is what the golden record
    below states.  Independently re-measured on this branch with a two-field probe,
    { a : 30; b : 4; }, which is 8 bytes on all four targets rather than the 5 or 8 a
    straddling rule would give.  The struct's NAME records the same fact.
  - each structure below carries no padding beyond the bytes the units themselves occupy, so
    its size is exactly the number of allocation units it spans -- 4 for a single 32-bit
    cover, 8 for the 64-bit cover, 12 for the three units nostraddle needs and for the three
    a pair of zero-width separators forces.

Every printed size, alignment and byte image follows from THAT premise.  Each of the four
supported ABIs fixes these choices in its own psABI document, and the measurement is the
statement that the four fix them identically; the printed values are target-invariant because
the four ABIs agree, not because the language required them to.

The consequence for the oracles.  Because the premise is measured to hold on all four
targets rather than assumed, no target restriction is applied and all three oracles remain
enabled, and a cross-backend divergence here is a FINDING -- a deliverable with a reproducer
and exact commands -- rather than something excused.  No expected-divergence marker is
attached, because no documented bitfield limitation exists anywhere in the repository to
serve as a basis.

What such a finding is ATTRIBUTABLE to is decided by the harness, from evidence the run
already holds, and this record does not decide it here.  Every cross-backend finding carries
the same-target reference capture beside the two it compared, and its manifest states one of:

  - attribution = abi-observation, when bcc AGREES with the toolchain that implements this
    target's own ABI and only the cross-target comparison differs.  A difference over a
    property each psABI fixes for itself is then a statement about two ABIs rather than a
    defect in bcc.  It remains a finding; it is not excused and not passed.
  - attribution = defect-candidate, when bcc disagrees with its own target's reference
    toolchain too, so two independent oracles point the same way.
  - attribution = undetermined, when no same-target reference capture accompanies the
    finding, in which case the target's psABI is authoritative and is what a maintainer
    reads next.

An earlier form of this record asserted flatly that a cross-backend divergence here is a
genuine defect rather than an implementation-defined difference, while FINDINGS.md described
a triage procedure under which same-target agreement makes it an ABI observation.  Both were
reasoning correctly about the same distinction and neither DECIDED it, so the two documents
could be read against each other.  The decision now lives in one place, in code, and both
documents point at it.

Signed straddling fields are observed through read-back of their values rather than through a
byte image, because the bit pattern of a negative value is a representation detail rather than a
value.  Their read-back values are fully defined and are compared across all four targets.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
